ount rests on faith in divine authority, not a change detected by the senses.","finding":"D5-matching-description"},{"world":"divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7","collection":"milestones","owner":"plain-divinity-not-sensory-proof","field":"text","before":"Tasting the bread cannot prove the change Aquinas describes, because he says its taste remains. His account rests on faith in divine authority, not on a hidden change detected by the senses.","after":"Aquinas says the bread becomes Christ’s body while its taste remains. Tasting it therefore cannot prove the change he claims. His account rests on faith in divine authority, not a change detected by the senses.","finding":"D5"},{"world":"divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7","collection":"items","owner":"ministry-not-sale","field":"reading.text","before":"A person can serve God without controlling God's gifts. Comparing Luther's limits on church authority with Aquinas's dependent instrument clarifies that distinction without making their doctrines identical.","after":"Luther says church ministers can announce God’s forgiveness but cannot make God forgive for money. Aquinas says baptism’s water acts through God’s power, not its own. Both distinguish serving God from independently controlling God’s gifts, although they address different practices.","finding":"D6"},{"world":"divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7","collection":"items","owner":"ministry-not-sale","field":"description","before":"A person can serve God without controlling God's gifts. Comparing Luther's limits on church authority with Aquinas's dependent instrument clarifies that distinction without making their doctrines identical.","after":"Luther says church ministers can announce God’s forgiveness but cannot make God forgive for money. Aquinas says baptism’s water acts through God’s power, not its own. Both distinguish serving God from independently controlling God’s gifts, although they address different practices.","finding":"D6-matching-description"},{"world":"d